---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import Reconstruction from '../components/Reconstruction.astro';

const FIGMA = 'https://www.figma.com/design/N7vnpK60xZfzI8luYKbevA/Alucify-Patterns?node-id=0-1';
const FIGMA_EMBED =
  'https://embed.figma.com/design/N7vnpK60xZfzI8luYKbevA/Alucify-Patterns?node-id=0-1&embed-host=share';

/** The three questions the case study answers, in the order it answers them. */
const questions = [
  ['Reach', 'How far did the change travel?'],
  ['Coverage', 'Which decisions did the agent address?'],
  ['Impact', 'Where does a person need to look?'],
];

const landing = [
  ['Blast radius', 'How much of the system may be affected?'],
  ['Agent hours', 'How much automated work sits behind the result?'],
  ['Token cost', 'What did this change cost to produce?'],
];

const severity = [
  ['Blast radius', 'How far the change reaches'],
  ['Regression risk', 'What existing behavior could break'],
  ['Data integrity', 'What stored or transferred information is exposed'],
  ['Backward compatibility', 'What existing use may no longer work'],
];

const flows = [
  ['First run', ['Sign in', 'Connect source', 'Build context', 'Ready']],
  ['Artifacts', ['Dashboard', 'Artifact', 'Audit', 'Re-audit']],
  ['Coverage', ['Dashboard', 'Coverage summary', 'Decision detail']],
  ['Impact', ['Dashboard', 'Impact summary', 'Concept detail']],
];

const related = [
  [
    '/ops-manager',
    'A product and the system it was built from',
    'A data-dense operations product and its inspectable component library.',
  ],
  [
    '/how-i-design-with-ai',
    'How I design with AI',
    'The operating rules behind AI-directed design work.',
  ],
  ['/review-loop', 'The review loop', 'Forty-eight stakeholder notes reviewed against running code.'],
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="Designing the review layer for AI-written code"
  metaTitle="Alucify — designing the review layer for AI-written code | Tyler Young"
  description="When an AI agent writes code, the reviewer inherits work they did not watch happen. Alucify reconstructs enough of it for a person to judge whether it should ship."
  eyebrow="01 · AI-Native Product"
  meta={[
    ['Product', 'Code intelligence for reviewing AI-written changes'],
    ['Contribution', 'Design · the designer on the team that built it'],
    ['Scope', '4 flows · 20 documented screens'],
    ['Status', 'Active venture · shown with the founder’s agreement'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="opening">
    When an AI agent writes code, the reviewer inherits work they did not watch
    happen. They need to reconstruct what changed, which decisions were made, where
    the risk sits, and what the work cost before they can approve it.
  </p>
  <p>
    I was the designer on the team that built Alucify. The work below is the design
    that makes that inherited work reviewable.
  </p>

  <ul class="strip">
    {
      questions.map(([label, q]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="strip-label">{label}</span>
          <span class="strip-note">{q}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <Reconstruction
    route="dashboard"
    label="Dashboard"
    title="Alucify dashboard reconstruction"
    caption="The dashboard establishes the review in one view: the artifact’s status, the completeness of its reasoning, and the expected impact of the change."
    eager
  />

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">01 · Orientation</p>
  <h2>The landing screen answers three questions</h2>
  <p>
    The first screen does not celebrate how much code the agent produced. It tells
    the reviewer how far the change reached, how much agent work it represents, and
    what it cost.
  </p>
  <p>
    Blast radius, agent hours and token cost sit beside coverage because all four
    affect the decision to approve the work. Cost also appears again on each
    individual change, beside the result it paid for — operational context rather
    than an accounting detail on another page.
  </p>

  <ul class="strip">
    {
      landing.map(([label, note]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="strip-label">{label}</span>
          <span class="strip-note">{note}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">02 · Coverage</p>
  <h2>A percentage is only useful if it can be opened</h2>
  <p>
    An overall coverage score is easy to scan and impossible to trust on its own.
    The design makes every decision behind it visible.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each row names a specific decision, categorises it as scope, architecture or
    data, and marks it covered, partial or not covered. The list sorts weakest
    first, so the gaps set the reviewer’s agenda rather than the successes.
  </p>
  <p>This turns coverage from a score into a review queue.</p>

  <Reconstruction
    route="coverage"
    label="Coverage analysis"
    title="Alucify coverage-analysis reconstruction"
    caption="The sample score is calculated from the 27 decisions shown below it. The table sorts incomplete decisions first, so the summary and its evidence cannot drift apart."
  />

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">03 · Severity</p>
  <h2>The interface raises its voice only when the risk does</h2>
  <p>Most of the product stays visually quiet. A critical change is the exception.</p>
  <p>
    Opening one reveals a written summary and a consistent breakdown across blast
    radius, regression risk, data integrity and backward compatibility. The reviewer
    can understand why the item is critical without decoding a colour or relying on
    a single score.
  </p>
  <p>
    Severity is carried by three signals together: a label, a visual marker, and a
    plain-language explanation.
  </p>

  <ul class="strip four">
    {
      severity.map(([label, note]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="strip-label">{label}</span>
          <span class="strip-note">{note}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <Reconstruction
    route="impact-detail"
    label="Impact detail"
    title="Alucify impact-detail reconstruction"
    caption="Open a concept to see the summary and its four severity dimensions. Previous and next controls move the reviewer through the queue without returning to the table."
  />

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">04 · System states</p>
  <h2>Trust is designed in the incomplete states</h2>
  <p>
    The full dashboard is only one moment in the product. The design covers twenty
    screens: first run, integration, daily review, stale results and re-audit.
  </p>
  <p>
    An analysis product has to explain not only what it knows, but when it is still
    working and when its answer should no longer be trusted.
  </p>

  <h3>When the answer is out of date</h3>
  <p>
    A stale audit does not quietly remain on screen as if it were current. The
    product states that the result has been deprecated and places the action that
    fixes it beside the warning.
  </p>

  <Reconstruction
    route="artifact-reaudit"
    label="Stale audit"
    title="Alucify stale-audit reconstruction"
    caption="The interface admits that its analysis is no longer current and pairs that admission with the re-audit action."
  />

  <h3>When the product is still working</h3>
  <p>
    Integration continues without requiring the user to supervise it. The state
    explains what is happening, what will end the wait, and how the user will know
    when the result is ready.
  </p>

  <Reconstruction
    route="in-progress"
    label="Integration in progress"
    title="Alucify integration-in-progress reconstruction"
    caption="The product explains the background work, promises an email when it is complete, and tells the user they can close the tab."
  />

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">05 · The complete system</p>
  <h2>Twenty screens across four connected flows</h2>
  <p>The product begins before the dashboard.</p>
  <p>
    The first flow moves from sign-in to a code context the product can reason
    about. The remaining three begin from the dashboard and branch into artifacts,
    coverage and impact. Returning those paths to one shared hub keeps the product
    understandable as its analysis deepens.
  </p>

  <div class="flows">
    <p class="sr-only">
      Four flows. First run goes from sign in, to connect source, to build context,
      to ready. The other three each begin at the dashboard: artifacts continues to
      artifact, then audit, then re-audit; coverage continues to the coverage
      summary and then a decision detail; impact continues to the impact summary and
      then a concept detail.
    </p>
    {
      flows.map(([name, steps]) => (
        <div class="flow" aria-hidden="true">
          <p class="flow-name">{name}</p>
          <ol class="flow-steps">
            {steps.map((step) => (
              <li class:list={['flow-step', { hub: step === 'Dashboard' }]}>{step}</li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <figure class="recon figma">
    <div class="recon-head">
      <span class="recon-label">Complete design file</span>
      <span class="recon-host">20 screens · 4 flows</span>
      <a class="recon-open" href={FIGMA} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        Open in Figma <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <div class="recon-viewport figma-viewport">
      <p class="recon-fallback">
        Complete design file — <a href={FIGMA} target="_blank" rel="noopener">open in Figma</a>
      </p>
      <iframe
        src={FIGMA_EMBED}
        title="The Alucify design file — twenty screens across four flows"
        loading="lazy"
        referrerpolicy="no-referrer"
        allowfullscreen></iframe>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      The complete file includes first run, the dashboard, artifact review, coverage
      analysis, impact analysis, and the loading, empty, stale and re-audit states
      between them.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">06 · Ownership</p>
  <h2>My role</h2>
  <p>
    I was the designer on the team that built Alucify. The product is the team's;
    the design is the part I did — the information architecture, the landing
    metrics, the coverage model, the severity system, and the states from first
    connection through daily review.
  </p>
  <p>
    That work required more than arranging data. It required deciding what evidence
    a reviewer needs, how summaries stay traceable to the decisions underneath them,
    and how the product communicates uncertainty without surrendering authority.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    The design problem was not helping an agent write more code. It was helping a
    person decide whether that code should ship.
  </p>

  <div class="disclosure">
    <p class="sec-eyebrow">About the public version</p>
    <h2>What you are looking at</h2>
    <p>This work is shown with the founder’s agreement.</p>
    <p>
      The screens above are a working reconstruction of the design, built so the
      states can be opened and used rather than viewed only as images. It is not the
      shipping product.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tenant, people and measurements are invented. The sample values reconcile
      because the summaries are computed from the rows beneath them; they are not
      customer results.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section slot="after" class="shell related" aria-labelledby="related-title">
    <p class="sec-eyebrow">Related</p>
    <h2 id="related-title" class="related-head">More AI-native product work</h2>
    <ul>
      {
        related.map(([href, label, note]) => (
          <li>
            <a class="artifact" href={href}>
              {label}
              <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </a>
            <span class="related-note">{note}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>
</DocumentLayout>
